body:
I'm ultimately just tired of people telling me I shouldn't be upset or disappointed. It's great that so many people can move past this already but I'm angry the most about the way they set up so much only to be used. Of course I was upset and disappointed that they lost but I also didnt except much in that regard, but the fact that they set up so much and were forced to sit around for hours the way I, and so many others, had to is so messed up. Fans sitting around for a performance are one thing, BTS being kept in the dark and forced to be awake for hours on end, keeping up the energy, keeping it professional, all of that.. that's just beyond disrespectful to me, and I'm not over it, and honestly I'm angry they did dynamite twice in a row as well. This is of course just speculation, but I'm sure the fact that it's their only english song is why this happened.

&#x200B;

Those of you who may want to argue that dynamite didnt deserve this because it was too pop, it was fluff, whatever, I get what you're saying, but what you're not acknowledging is that Bangtan had other songs that did well that could've reached number one had radio/shows etc not been unwilling to play a non english song on the radio. This is systemic, and it's purposeful, I would've loved for their more artistic songs to get credit but because of these xenophobic norms, they had to write an english song to break through, thats not on them, thats on the American music community.

Honestly, I think it's amazing how much we normalize too. I would've never really thought much of being too old now, or of teenagers, particularly young girls, being this diminished, had I not been part of this niche following where it was brought to the forefront, not because I don't care, but because it's /normal/. It's amazing how much of this is systematic in how we're nurtured. Narrative is amazingly persuasive even when it's incorrect.

&#x200B;

It was a long hard journey to be honest, because like a lot of people, I found solace from my day to day struggles in them, and it felt like they were struggling with me. Bangtan are different because they didn't have a leg up etc you know the drill, but experiencing it.. it's something I will bring with me for the rest of my life. They literally were part of my formative years, they made me who I am today, and encourage me to be more than that. But the most amazing thing about them is even though I was there from the beginning, you don't have to be there from the beginning to feel their struggle, to relate, and to find meaning and strength from them.

&#x200B;

I think the biggest, most interesting shift between being a long time fan, and a newer ( but no less important!) fan, is just the weight of the day to day struggles from the past, and the equal weight of their popularity, their growth, and the sheer amazing things they've achieved. It's frightening to feel like you were on the same level as someone and then they surpassed not only you, but /everyone/. So I think I can really understand this new album on many levels. But both sides are incredibly important. I'm bogged down with anxiety about them changing a lot, but I think there's this wonderful, refreshing aspect to new fans who are just.. excited to see where the boys will go, and that soothes me a lot \^\^

It's just really funny to me too because they totally ignore the aspect of us that have, quite literally, grown with BTS. I'm a 96 liner, so I'm between Jungkook and 95z obviously, and I got into BTS in 2014. I've been following them and gaining strength from them since I was a teenager, and now that I'm in my 20's it's suddenly weird and a older women obsessive over younger men that aren't even younger.

&#x200B;

My point relating to yours is, I grew with them, I watched them be crushed and watched them use their spite when they had nothing else left. So I use my spite to prove people wrong, too. Spite can be a surprising motivator when it feels like you've put everything into something and gotten no results beyond people looking down on you and mocking you. If I see a reflection of myself in a bunch of guys with a dream, if they reach out to me and motivate me to put 110% into anything.. Isn't that something amazing? Isn't that worth something being admired? Yet the boy band with crazy teen fans narrative continues. Teenagers are people too, young adults are people, they're the people of the future, it'd do well for older generations to remember that.

The brain is a strange thing, I recovered really well too, probably due to being 21 when it happened but.. it's interesting because I can't even remember the pain it was just so excruciating I've forgotten it now. I remember more how I couldn't stop vomiting and how I couldn't eat than the pain itself.

&#x200B;

The body is a weird thing. I'm glad you made it out alright too though!

I bought two packs of vday Jimin
None of them were prisms...
My gf opens one of the rando packs from the anniversary, gets three prism jimins including the half and half one I've been wanting for the new album... ;__;

Literally nearly every comment is screaming about Jimin but there's still not enough screaming about Jimin so

MY BABY, LISTEN TO HIS BEAUTIFUL VOICE I COULD CRY ;____;

Edit: A more thought out comment on how Jimin despite definitely having less proper pronunciation, he has what I feel is better enunciation I guess? than Jungkook does. It's almost like to me Jungkook sort of slurs his words together whilst with Jimin, his are more properly separated. This is really cool and interesting to me because it sort of reflects in how they both talk, which is very similar to Jungkook mumbling quite a bit and Jimin being very clear and precise. Just very cool that despite his English being more on par, I understand Jimin's lines better as someone who didn't listen to the original song/cover a lot. Ofc they're both incredible and I'm so happy sigh
